UberSDR: let a spectrum channel's bin count grow without corrupting the heap

WHAT
  Track the allocated length of chan->spectrum.bin_data in a new field and grow
  the buffer when the requested bin count outgrows it, instead of inferring the
  need from a local that cannot differ.

WHY
  In demod_spectrum() (src/spectrum.c) the buffer guard reads:

      if(chan->spectrum.bin_data == NULL || chan->spectrum.bin_count != bin_count)

  but the local `bin_count` is synced to chan->spectrum.bin_count earlier in the
  same iteration -- in the `fft_n <= 0` reinitialization block, which a bin count
  change is itself what triggers -- and again at the bottom of every loop.  The
  two are therefore always equal by the time the guard runs, so only the NULL arm
  can ever fire.  bin_data is allocated exactly once, at whatever bin count the
  channel was created with, and stays that size forever.

  narrowband_poll() and wideband_poll() then memset and fill bin_count elements
  of it, and radio_status.c encodes bin_count of them.  So raising a live
  channel's BIN_COUNT writes past the end of the block: with glibc that surfaces
  as "corrupted size vs. prev_size in fastbins" and radiod dies, taking every
  channel with it.

  Lowering a bin count is safe (the buffer is merely oversized), which is why
  this has stayed latent -- every existing client either holds its bin count
  fixed or only ever reduces it.

WHY CONFIG CANNOT DO THIS
  It is a heap-sizing bug in the demodulator, reachable from any BIN_COUNT
  command.  No configuration key affects it.

WHY UBERSDR CANNOT DO THIS
  Honestly: it could work around it, and did, with a per-SSRC ceiling recorded at
  channel creation (spectrumBinCeiling, now removed).  A channel created with the
  largest bin count it will ever need can shrink and regrow freely within that.
  The cost is that the ceiling has to be chosen before the zoom that needs it is
  known, and bins are float32 on the wire -- so every channel would carry its
  deepest zoom's payload at every zoom, four bytes per bin per poll, whether or
  not the view had any use for them.  With the guard fixed, bin_count follows the
  view: 1,172 bins for a 234 kHz span, 1,024 for a 117 kHz one, and nothing pays
  for a zoom it is not at.

  The bug is also a live remote-triggerable heap overflow either way.

WHAT WOULD LET US DROP IT
  Upstream fixing the guard.  Worth sending: this is an upstream defect, not a
  divergence we want, and anything that grows a spectrum channel's bin count hits
  it.

SECONDARY
  The same block set `void *old = chan->spectrum.ring` before realloc'ing
  bin_data, so the OOM path freed the ring buffer and leaked the old bin_data.
  Corrected to bin_data while rewriting these lines.  The path ends in `goto
  quit`, which tears the channel down regardless, so this was cosmetic.

Applies to ka9q-radio cce087e2090ed71889697f3831f34ef7825d9a44.
Verified: spectrum.c compiles warning-free under the project's own flags
  (-std=gnu11 -Wall -Wextra -fcx-limited-range -D_GNU_SOURCE=1); radio.c,
  radio_status.c, modes.c, control.c and monitor-data.c all syntax-check clean
  against the added field.  NOT yet exercised against a running radiod -- the
  first deployment should watch for the abort this is meant to prevent while
  actually raising a live channel's bin count.

diff --git a/src/radio.h b/src/radio.h
index fd2e6a94..9c77ba3d 100644
--- a/src/radio.h
+++ b/src/radio.h
@@ -259,6 +259,7 @@ struct channel {
     double noise_bw;  // Estimated noise bandwidth of bin with current window
     int bin_count;    // Requested bin count
     float *bin_data;  // Array of real floats with bin_count elements
+    int bin_data_size; // Elements actually allocated in bin_data; >= bin_count
     double crossover;  // Crossover frequency between algorithms, Hz
     double shape;     // Analysis window parameter if any (kaiser β, gaussian σ)
     int fft_n;        // size of analysis FFT
diff --git a/src/spectrum.c b/src/spectrum.c
index b3577f6c..a0d93f5e 100644
--- a/src/spectrum.c
+++ b/src/spectrum.c
@@ -155,13 +155,24 @@ int demod_spectrum(void *arg){
     }
     if(response_needed){      // Generate new bin data for the next response
       // Make sure output frequency bin data buffers exist
-      if(chan->spectrum.bin_data == NULL || chan->spectrum.bin_count != bin_count){
-	void *old = chan->spectrum.ring;
+      /* Grow the bin buffer whenever the requested bin count outgrows what was
+	 allocated.  This used to compare chan->spectrum.bin_count against the
+	 local bin_count, but that local is synced to it earlier in this same
+	 iteration (in the fft_n <= 0 reinitialization above) and again at the
+	 bottom of the loop, so the two are always equal here and only the NULL
+	 arm could ever fire.  bin_data therefore stayed at whatever size it was
+	 first allocated at while the polls below memset and fill bin_count
+	 elements, so raising a live channel's bin count wrote past the end of
+	 the block.  Track the allocation instead of inferring it. */
+      if(chan->spectrum.bin_data == NULL || chan->spectrum.bin_data_size < chan->spectrum.bin_count){
+	void *old = chan->spectrum.bin_data;
 	chan->spectrum.bin_data = realloc(chan->spectrum.bin_data, chan->spectrum.bin_count * sizeof *chan->spectrum.bin_data);
 	if(chan->spectrum.bin_data == NULL){
+	  chan->spectrum.bin_data_size = 0;
 	  FREE(old); // emulate reallocf()
 	  goto quit;
 	}
+	chan->spectrum.bin_data_size = chan->spectrum.bin_count;
       }
       if(chan->spectrum.rbw <= chan->spectrum.crossover){
 #if 0
@@ -198,6 +209,7 @@ int demod_spectrum(void *arg){
   destroy_plan(&chan->spectrum.plan);
   FREE(chan->spectrum.window);
   FREE(chan->spectrum.bin_data);
+  chan->spectrum.bin_data_size = 0;
   FREE(chan->spectrum.ring);
   chan->spectrum.ring_size = 0;
   return chan->demod_type == INVALID_DEMOD ? -1 : 0;
